d like the  heart of the story is this section why  do we have all this other stuff but you  stick it in there and a biographical  picture because well that was part of  his life and so I think structurally a  lot of them just fall apart from me for  that reason because I think the fact  that it's a true story is often used as  a crutch especially considering how much  of this stuff gets changed anyways so  yeah I was a little bit nervous because  I like Queen I like Queen a lot Queen is  one of my favorite bands if I'm being  completely honest but I really liked  this and that was also doubly surprising  because I'd seen more than a few people  including some online critics who I  really like and respect not caring for  the movie and pointing out some serious  storytelling flaws or technical issues  like editing being really wonky and some  of the scenes and things like that and  I'm not gonna dispute things like that  but here's what I'll say I'm really good  and I might need to think about doing  this more often if I think I can  normally what I do a review I have a  tendency to do it within a few hours of  my having seen whatever the thing is in  this case I'm recording this too two or  three days after I saw it two days there  we go  two days after I saw it and that's  actually given me made me more kind to  it because what I'm left with at this  point is the technical flaws of it which  I can acknowledge have kind of faded in  my memory and what I'm left with when I  think about the film is great music  charismatic performers not only from the  lead Remy Malick who is phenomenal as  Freddie Mercury and what feels like a  story worth telling so I think I I'm not  sure that I'm gonna  like gonna start defaulting to reviewing  these a few days later and also there's  some films that are like if I don't talk  about them quick the details are gonna  fade from my head because they were so  unmemorable usually that's if I got a  rant about something but with something  like this its successes are rooted in  how it makes you feel as opposed to the  technical nuts and bolts of the  filmmaking which is not really  exceptional it's not awful I don't think  but it's not the best in the world not  by any means so I think in terms of like  the accolades that it's getting I get  why the rally Malick deserves a  nomination I'm not sure the film does as  a whole but that's not to say that the  film isn't good because I do think  especially if you like Queen that the  film is worth watching and I actually I  was impressed at how much wasn't  streamlined or minimized out of it bill  I was expecting like nothing more than  minimal acknowledgement at the fact that  he's Percy and he was born in Zanzibar  and I feel like that's something that  like a lot of people don't even have an  awareness of and it was nice to see that  like right off the bat one of the first  things we get about him as a character  which was pretty cool there's also his  relationship with the character of Mary  that I thought was really interesting  and again I'm not gonna speak to the  accuracy of this because I I don't know  in the amount of research I'm prepared  to do to find out how accurate it was or  wasn't is very limited so let me just  make very really clear if anyone doesn't  like this movie because of the  alterations it makes to the facts I yeah  I'm not even gonna argue with you on  that but in terms of the film as a  self-contained thing his dynamic with  Mary was really fascinating and I  thought really quite touching because he  really did care about her but at the  same time was not able to love her in a  way that she kind of needed in her life  yet at the same time he was envious of  the people who could love her in that  way and there's a lot of emphasis on how  lonely Freddy is and some of that they  do sort of they at least I felt that the  film did a good balancing act of not  making it seem like he was lonely  because of his sexuality but how his  sexuality at the time in which he was  living fed into his loneliness I think  at least for me and my takeaway I I  thought it did a good job of balancing  that so that it wasn't equate ingest you  know not being straight with being a  lonely unhappy person it it didn't feel  like it was doing a one-to-one with that  which I appreciated seeing some of the  you know creative stuff behind the songs  was well I think that if there was if  there was something that was cringy it  was probably Mike Myers as part as an  EMI executive who I think I did read  didn't even exist he's like a proxy for  a guy who did exist but he's  exaggerating for the sake of a joke and  so that they could have Mike Myers  famous for being Elaine in Wayne's World  head-banging to Bohemian Rhapsody tell  Freddie Mercury and Queen we can't  release both me and Rhapsody as a single  because people can't bang their heads in  the car to it like that that that was  cringe right there that that was  unnecessary and it didn't help and it  and honestly the film kind of ground to  a halt so that they could have that  scene that's that's like one of the few  criticisms that I had at the time  watching it that stuck with me because  thinking back on I can think of a number  of things or at least I can remember  thinking a number of things that weren't  lining up and weren't clicking for me as  I was watching but the thing is at this  point a couple days later I can't  remember what those were and even like  what they might have related to what I'm  left with overwhelmingly is the stronger  feeling and I think the saw the film  does use the songs of Queen quite well  especially in the climax the performance  at Live Aid is basically serves as the  climax of the film and I think that is  done really really wonderfully and I  think the the buildup  to it the catharsis of it the  performance of it it makes it feel  triumphant and it really is I think a  good point to choose to bring the  narrative to a clos